@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title cursor paradigm — policy-gated autonomous loop (layered enforcement)

' covers: defs/cursor/entrypoint.sh
' covers: packages/lib/entrypoint-lib.sh
'
' ── NO PROVEO DENY LIST (2026-10-24, user: "Keep upstream, lose the deny list")
' The sbx cursor kit seeds ~/.cursor/cli-config.json as
' {"network":{"useHttp1ForAgent":true}} (lifecycle files:, overwrite:false);
' proveo copies that entry with the kit (WithLifecycle keeps `files`).
' defs/cursor/defaults/cli-config.json and its permissions.deny baseline are
' deleted; the entrypoint no longer seeds or counts deny rules. Layer 1 keeps
' the root-owned hooks and readonly subagents; an operator's own deny entries
' in cli-config.json or a project .cursor/cli.json still apply. Pinned by
' TestCursorShipsNoDenyListDefault (internal/contract/defaults_test.go).
'
' ── THREE ENTRYPOINT DETAILS WORTH KEEPING ─────────────────────────────────
' PROXY COMPATIBILITY is two changes, not one. Beside the nested
' `network.useHttp1ForAgent` written into cli-config.json, the entrypoint also
' exports NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 — the config tells cursor-agent HOW to speak, the
' env var is what makes node honour HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY. Both are
' applied only when one of the four proxy variables is actually set.
'
' A CALLER'S OWN --output-format WINS. It is a parse contract, not a preference:
' something downstream is reading that stream, so the evidence step adds
' `--output-format stream-json --stream-partial-output` only in headless mode
' AND only when the caller named no format of its own. In interactive mode the
' step says so out loud — cursor-agent exposes no verbosity flag there — rather
' than silently doing nothing.
'
' HEADLESS RUNS NEED --trust UP FRONT, because there is no prompt to answer.
' Headless is detected by scanning argv for `-p` / `--print`.
'
' AUTH IS CURSOR-ONLY. All inference transits the Cursor backend, so there is no
' provider-key or local-model alternative and the no-key warning names the one
' path that works: CURSOR_API_KEY from cursor.com/dashboard → API Keys, or
' `agent login` interactively (NO_OPEN_BROWSER=1 prints the URL instead of
' trying to open one). CURSOR_API_KEY is PREFERRED because login tokens are
' scrubbed from the durable cache, so an interactive login does not persist.
'
' Utility subcommands (login, logout, status, whoami, ls, resume, update,
' upgrade, mcp, create-chat, uninstall, help, -v/--version, -h/--help) exec
' straight through with none of the autonomy flags attached.
'
actor "Developer" as DEV

node "cursor container" as CTR <<host>> {
  component "defs/cursor/entrypoint.sh" as EP <<app>>
  component "cursor-agent --force" as CLI <<app>>

  frame "Layer 1 — in-process policy" as L1 <<host>> {
    component "operator's own permissions.deny\n(proveo seeds none)" as DENY <<error>>
    component "hooks.json\nallow / deny / ask on tool events" as HOOKS <<error>>
    component "subagents with native readonly" as RO <<app>>
  }

  database "/app — repo" as WS <<db>>
}

node "Layer 2 — container + egress" as L2 <<host>> {
  component "hardened container\ncap-drop · no-new-privileges · pids-limit" as SBX <<app>>
  component "broker egress\n(vendor-pinned inference)" as EG <<app>>
}

cloud "api2.cursor.sh\npinned TLS" as VENDOR <<cloud>>

DEV ARROW_MAIN CLI
CLI ARROW_MAIN DENY : every tool call evaluated
DENY ARROW_ERROR CLI : errorLabel(deny wins over --force)
CLI ARROW_MAIN HOOKS
HOOKS ARROW_ERROR CLI : errorLabel(root-owned — out-ranks the agent)
CLI ARROW_MAIN WS
CTR ARROW_MAIN SBX
SBX ARROW_CLOUD VENDOR : via EG

note right of L1
  **Enforcement is layered, and ""--force"" only removes
  the interactive prompt.** An operator's ""permissions.deny""
  entries are hard stops (proveo seeds none); the enterprise hook layer is root-owned at
  ""/etc/cursor/hooks.json"" — highest precedence
  (enterprise > team > project > user), so the sandboxed
  agent can neither edit nor out-rank it.

  The harness never trusts prompt-level compliance where
  a structural control exists.
end note

note bottom of L2
  Cursor's own OS sandbox (Landlock/seccomp) is disabled
  on purpose — unprivileged userns fail in this container.
  Docker is the FS/process boundary today; network
  containment is deferred to the free-tier ""sbx"" experiment
  (_spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox.puml), not Landlock.
end note

note bottom of VENDOR
  **Vendor-pinned inference.** No custom base URL, so
  ""--local-model"" is rejected. Since 2026-10-16 sbx is the
  only backend and its proxy brokers ""CURSOR_API_KEY""
  natively; the key goes to sbx's secret store
  (TestCursorBrokersThroughTheSbxStore). The provider entry
  maps that key to "".cursor.sh"" / "".cursor.com"".

  **HISTORY — what an intercepting tier looked like from inside the
  docker container (deleted 2026-10-16).** ""cursor-agent""'s TLS to ""api2\.cursor\.sh""
  is pinned, so the MITM hop cannot be made to carry the
  real header: the allowlist tier (//firewall//) hands the
  agent the ""proveo-brokered"" sentinel and the review tier
  (//proxy//) withholds the key altogether. Either way the
  CLI reports //"invalid API key"// — an error that blames
  the credential rather than the tier that removed it, so
  the operator cannot tell a revoked key from a proveo
  decision.

  **There is no bring-your-own-key path, and now the prompt says so.**
  Confirmed by Cursor staff on the forum ('Can I use provider API keys with
  Cursor cli agent?'), answered //Cursor CLI doesn't support BYOK with your own
  provider API keys yet ... the CLI only works with models through your Cursor
  subscription//. The auth row draws ""usage credits"" anyway and gates it
  with that reason: dropping the option silently is what left 'cursor ignores
  my ANTHROPIC_API_KEY' as something an operator could only find out by
  running. The manifest's ""providers: [cursor]"" is the machine-readable half
  of the same fact.

**Metered and plan are BOTH real here, and the split is Cursor's to make.**
  One ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" spends the plan's included usage first and
  usage-based overage after, on Cursor's side, where proveo can see neither.
  So cursor's models carry no billing verdict and the auth row offers both
  sides while saying outright that it cannot decide between them.

  Gating the usage option was wrong twice over: cursor CAN be billed metered,
  and an operator whose ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" does nothing here still needs to
  be told why. "Usage credits" means two different things —
  bring-your-own-key, which cursor lacks, and metered spend on the vendor's
  own gateway, which it has. ""VendorPinnedWhy"" now explains the BYOK half
  alone.

    Cursor's key is a PLAN credential despite the name — the registry marks it
  ""Harness: cursor"", so no other harness is offered it as a spendable key
  either. SPEC: _spec/internal/credentials/credential-decisions.puml

  **The refusal used to speak claudecode's words.** On the sbx backend a
  subscription harness with no declared credential was refused with a hardcoded
  string: 'Mint a token on the host and export it: claude setup-token'. A
  cursor run with no ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" got exactly that — instructions for a
  different product, naming a variable cursor never reads. It now builds from
  ""SubscriptionAuthHints"" for the harness it actually refused, and offers the
  'export a provider key instead' line only where one would work.

  **HISTORY — the posture was declared, not defaulted per-flag.**
  cursor's manifest declared ""capabilities: egress:[open] ·
  credentials:[forward] · providers:[cursor]"" for the docker
  backend. DECIDED 2026-10-16 ("Drop the pins"): the
  ""egress"" and ""credentials"" pins are gone, and so is the
  top-level ""provider: cursor"" key (MEASURED: only
  ""capabilities.providers: [cursor]"" pins the vendor now), and
  ""--egress-mode"" is an unknown flag (2026-10-17).

  **On sbx the broker is native, not an interception.**
  sbx's proxy attaches ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" itself, so broker
  stands whether defaulted or named, and the picker keeps
  forward off (operator decision 2026-09-29). With the pins
  gone, the "always brokers natively" help on the broker option
  no longer fires for cursor (it keys on a credentials cap).
end note

note bottom of EP
  Seeds user-level config into ""${CURSOR_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.cursor}""
  from baked defaults, never mutating the mounted
  workspace; detects a proxied environment and sets
  ""useHttp1ForAgent"" (agent traffic is HTTP/2 streaming by
  default, which the proxy hop cannot carry).
end note
note bottom of CLI
  **The CLI is installed system-wide under a root-owned prefix**, by
  pointing HOME at ""/opt/cursor-dist"" for the install only. Two properties
  follow: the agent, running as the host uid, cannot tamper with its own
  binary, and the CLI's runtime auto-update cannot write there either. The
  image is immutable — updating the CLI means rebuilding it.

  There is no documented pinning variable, so the installer resolves
  whatever release is current; ""CURSOR_INSTALL_URL"" points it at a mirrored
  copy, and real pinning means fetching a versioned tarball yourself.

  Pre-baked defaults (deny config, read-only reviewer subagents, the loop
  rule) are seeded into ""~/.cursor"" on first run, or re-seeded with
  ""CURSOR_RESEED=1"". The loop rule reaches the workspace only when asked
  for explicitly.

  **Evidence is limited by the CLI, not by policy.** There is no verbosity
  switch for the interactive TUI; the only dial is the headless output
  format, where ""stream-json"" emits each tool call and result as it happens
  (plain text reports just the final message) and
  ""--stream-partial-output"" adds text deltas. Thinking events are
  suppressed in print mode whatever is passed — so verbose here buys tool
  calls and diffs, never reasoning.
end note

note bottom of SBX
  **Cursor's own Landlock sandbox is disabled deliberately.** Landlock
  needs an unprivileged user namespace, which a cap-dropped container
  cannot provide, so enabling it would fail rather than confine. Docker is
  the filesystem and process boundary here; network containment is the
  egress tier. Overridable with ""PROVEO_CURSOR_SANDBOX"" — see
  ""_spec/_experiments/docker-sandbox\.puml"".

  **LSP arrives over MCP because cursor has no external-LSP config.** The
  ""mcp-language-server"" bridge runs one instance per language server, and
  the bridge plus gopls are built as **static** binaries so a Go runtime
  never ships in the final image. The shared detector picks only the
  languages the workspace uses //and// the image installed.
end note


@enduml
